@page "/mechanism/7-streams"

<PageTitle>7. Stream references — JS interop demos</PageTitle>

<div class="mechanism-page">
    <MechanismNav />

    <header class="mech-head">
        <p class="mech-kicker">
            Mechanism 7 of @Mechanisms.All.Count · you are reading this in
            <span class="host-chip">@RuntimeMode.Name</span> — the same page in
            <CounterpartLink />
        </p>
        <h1>Stream references: passing large data</h1>
        <p class="mech-lede">
            Move a payload as binary instead of materialising it as a JSON string on both sides. The
            two directions use different types and are not symmetric — that asymmetry is the trap.
        </p>
        <p class="demo-support">
            Available in: <strong>@Mechanisms.Availability(7)</strong>
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>Two directions, two different APIs</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>JavaScript to .NET.</strong> The JavaScript function returns the bytes, you ask for
            them as an <code>IJSStreamReference</code>, and then you open it:
            <code>OpenReadStreamAsync(maxAllowedSize: …)</code>. The data is the return value, and the
            size cap is a parameter on <em>your</em> side.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>.NET to JavaScript.</strong> You construct a <code>DotNetStreamReference</code> and
            pass it as an <em>argument</em>; the JavaScript side chooses the shape it wants,
            <code>await streamRef.arrayBuffer()</code> or <code>await streamRef.stream()</code>. Here
            there is no size cap to raise, because you own the stream.
        </p>
        <p>
            Two behaviours that surprise people: transmission starts as soon as you pass the reference
            into an interop call, even if the JavaScript never reads it; and disposal is usually not
            your problem, because <code>leaveOpen: false</code> releases the underlying stream for you.
            Flip it to <code>true</code> and the stream becomes your responsibility, including the risk
            of disposing it mid-transmission.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>What the panels print in @RuntimeMode.Name</h2>
        <p class="mech-out-label">✓ JavaScript to .NET, with the size cap raised</p>
        <pre class="mech-out">1000000 bytes arrived as binary, without a Base64 string on either side.</pre>

        <p class="mech-out-label">✓ .NET to JavaScript, as a DotNetStreamReference</p>
        <pre class="mech-out">JavaScript received 250000 bytes from .NET</pre>

        <p class="mech-out-label">✗ Same payload, forget maxAllowedSize</p>
        @if (RuntimeMode.IsWebAssembly)
        {
            <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">ArgumentOutOfRangeException: The incoming data stream of length 1000000
exceeds the maximum allowed length 512000. Arg_ParamName_Name, maxAllowedSize</pre>
        }
        else
        {
            <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">ArgumentOutOfRangeException: The incoming data stream of length 1000000
exceeds the maximum allowed length 512000. (Parameter 'maxAllowedSize')</pre>
        }

        <p class="mech-note">
            The default cap is <strong>512,000 bytes</strong>, and it fails at run time on a call that
            looks completely ordinary at compile time. The payload here is a megabyte, so dropping the
            argument is enough to reproduce it. The tail of the message is again host-specific:
            WebAssembly prints the resource key <code>Arg_ParamName_Name</code>, Blazor Server prints
            the formatted <code>(Parameter '…')</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>Why a cap at all</h2>
        <p>
            The stream is data the other side chose to send. Without a ceiling you asked for, a
            JavaScript function that returns a 2 GB buffer would be accepted happily, and the memory
            would be spent before you noticed. The cap is the only place where a stream call states how
            much you are prepared to receive — so set it from your expected payload, not from a
            comfortable default.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-split">
        <div class="mech-use">
            <h2>Use it when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You need megabytes of binary data across the boundary without the memory spike of
                    Base64-encoded JSON.</li>
                <li>The payload arrives as a stream already — a file, a network body, a generated
                    texture.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mech-never">
            <h2>Never when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>The payload comfortably fits a normal call. For small JSON,
                    <code>InvokeAsync</code> is simpler and fast enough — measure first.</li>
                <li>You are streaming text you will deserialise anyway. That is two slow steps instead
                    of one.</li>
                <li>You only need the bytes and can hold them in memory: a byte-array call avoids
                    Base64 without the stream ceremony.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="mech-live-head">The live panels</h2>
    <CascadingValue Name="MechanismPageOwnsHeading" Value="true">
        <Demo07Streams />
    </CascadingValue>

    <MechanismPager Number="7" />
</div>
